WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.200
An estimated 17.3 million adults and 3.2 million adolescents have depression in America alone. 

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:10.180
People suffering from depression experience fatigue, lack of concentration,

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:13.160
loss of interest in hobbies, and irritability. 

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:16.130
Many feel too ashamed or hopeless to reach out for help. 

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:20.020
However, depression is treatable. Approaches like exercise, 

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:23.170
talk therapy, and medication can alleviate symptoms of depression. 

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:28.100
For many, a class of drugs called Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors, 

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:34.220
a.k.a SSRIs, drastically improve their quality of life by sustaining serotonin levels in the brain. 

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:38.180
Serotonin is a neurotransmitter that neurons use to relay signals 

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:42.210
and it plays a significant role in mood, sleep, and appetite regulation. 

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:48.010
When a pre-neuron releases Serotonin into the synaptic cleft, the space between the two neurons,

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:51.230
it binds to Serotonin G-coupled receptors on the next neuron. 

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:55.060
This activates the different G-proteins which get separated 

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:59.080
and trigger a cascade of depolarization events that pass the signal on. 

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:02.210
Once the message is conveyed, transmembrane serotonin transporters 

00:01:02.215 --> 00:01:08.100
of the presynaptic neuron reabsorb Serotonin to avoid resending the same message. 

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:12.070
Patients with depression have relatively low amounts of serotonin. 

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:17.230
SSRIs help increase serotonin levels by targeting serotonin reabsorption. 

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.090
But how does it work? 

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:24.150
First, SSRIs bind to an allosteric site on the serotonin transporter.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.160
This causes a change in conformation, inactivating the transporter 

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:33.020
and blocking the tunnel where serotonin is reabsorbed. As a result, 

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:36.190
serotonin remains in the synaptic cleft for a longer period of time 

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:40.010
where it continues to interact with the receptors, signaling the 

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.210
transduction pathways and prolonging its effects on the body. 

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:48.030
Through treatments like SSRIs, many have overcome depression. 

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:52.050
Depression is a medical condition, and there’s no shame in seeking help. 

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:55.090
If you or someone you know is struggling with depression, 

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.150
please visit the many available resources in-person and online.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.130
Thank you.

